  "body": "One of the things that came up in w3ctag/design-reviews#213 is that recursive fetching is something that exists across multiple resource types.  One of them is JS modules (which was the subject in that issue), another is CSS stylesheets.  In at least the stylesheet case, some of the subresources are of different types (e.g., images, maybe fonts... although font fetching is lazy and thus probably shouldn't be part of recursive loading).\r\n\r\nAs part of that discussion, we were wondering if `rel=preload` should support recursive fetching more generally (perhaps based on the `as` attribute) so that developers using preloading don't need to enumerate all of the subresources of something.  This is easier for the developer, although it might mean that the preloading is slower because it needs to wait for network roundtrips in order to start some of the preloads.  (On the other hand, it may well still be faster than *not* preloading the subresources, and is likely much easier to maintain.)\r\n\r\nOr are there other reasons that recursive fetching is valuable for modules but not for other preloadable resource types that have subresources?",
